  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places trough/front dropping through\nMinnesota, with high over the Alberta/Saskatchewan border\nstretching into Montana. Upper level analysis places compact low\nover the southern Manitoba/Ontario border, with high over New\nMexico and ridge stretching north into the Northern Rockies. \nOver our area, gusty northwesterly winds continue between the \naforementioned surface systems, while scattered to numerous \nclouds pass through cyclonic flow aloft behind the upper low. \n\nFor this evening, a quiet night is on tap as upper low pushes\noff to the east and surface high starts to settle in. \n\nOn Wednesday, quiet weather continues with upper ridge slowly\nmeandering a bit to the east and surface high passing over,\nresulting in a pleasant day. Thursday will see milder\ntemperatures as ridge continues to push east.\n\nChances for precipitation start to enter the forecast Friday as\nridge continues to meander east and a surface low develops to\nthe lee of the Rockies, bringing increasing south-southeasterly\nwinds and increased moisture to the area. Latest model guidance\nsuggest that severe weather may be a possibility given strong\ndeep layer shear and increased instability, which is also backed\nby NSSL & CSU machine learning guidance.  \n\nLow to medium chances for showers/storms continue through the\nweekend into the start of the upcoming week as we remain in a\ngenerally westerly to west-southwesterly upper flow pattern with\na variety of disturbances moving through.",
